b/ref 1061 17243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa6e7f35-addb-57cf-a360-9e6406254399" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1061 17244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2fd97122-b858-5040-a119-fad68a3d03fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1061 17245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "da013532-9009-5401-a121-9e5bc3920d43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134605 #edb/ref 1061 17246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec9418ab-c448-5510-808b-5f8b1a2094a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1064 "Applying a row operation to any $3\\times 3$ matrix is the same as *pre-multiplying* (multiplying on the left) the matrix by the corresponding elementary matrix.\n\nFor instance, if we wish to swap the first row and the second row of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ we pre-multiply $A$ by the corresponding elementary matrix \n![](images/2d/2de2ee02cbd3b00b6945fc8df8d84e83920fc0889c76394c1c726c94d68b1d99.svg)\nto get $E_1 A.$\n\nTo perform a sequence of row operations to a $3\\times 3$ matrix, we pre-multiply the matrix by the elementary matrix corresponding to the first operation, and then pre-multiply the result by the elementary matrix corresponding to the second operation.\n\nFor instance, given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ suppose that we would like to multiply the second row by $2$ and then add $3$ times the first row to the second row. To do this, we first pre-multiply $A$ by \n![](images/61/61f42ffa5ee0defeb3330421ead9c42f84200b274577c96cffe1d59ed9cf5cef.svg)\nto get $E_2 A.$ Then we pre-multiply the result by \n![](images/be/be5d3785649ca7ac1557b43ca434c2558af417d29db47c10ef2938c1099db6f5.svg) \nto get $E_3 E_2 A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1065 3171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c110a2ca-8856-5b26-9ded-2e9f35023747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135024 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428583 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428583 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2089->1726. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428583 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050576 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428584 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428584 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: linear=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no 